OTP send endpoint returns success even when email delivery fails
## Bug Description
The OTP send endpoint sends emails asynchronously via callback but never waits for delivery success before storing the OTP and returning a success response.

## Affected File
`backend/resources/auth/otpResource.js:40-53`

## Problem
```javascript
// Lines 40-49: Email send is callback-based, not awaited
transporter.sendMail(mailOptions, (error, info) => {
    if (error) {
        console.error(error);
        // Don't return here, request might have timed out? 
        // Or should we return error to client?
    } else {
        console.log("Message sent: %s", info.response);
    }
});

// Lines 52-53: OTP stored IMMEDIATELY regardless of email delivery
await Otp.deleteMany({ email });
await Otp.create({ email, otp });

return res.status(200).json({ status: true, message: `${messages.otpSent} to ${email}` });
```

The email send is fire-and-forget. If the SMTP server rejects the email, the OTP is still stored in the database and the user receives a "success" response. The user then tries the OTP they never received, causing confusion.

Additionally, there's no validation that the `loginForRole` from the request body matches the user's actual roles before sending an OTP (lines 26-34 skip this check when the user doesn't exist).

## Steps to Reproduce
1. Use the `/send-otp` endpoint with a valid email
2. If the SMTP server is misconfigured or the email bounces
3. The endpoint still returns `{"status": true, "message": "OTP Sent to user@..."}`
4. The user waits for an OTP that never arrives

## Proposed Fix
Wrap `sendMail` in a Promise and only store the OTP if delivery succeeds:

```javascript
const sendMail = (options) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    transporter.sendMail(options, (error, info) => {
        if (error) reject(error);
        else resolve(info);
    });
});

try {
    await sendMail(mailOptions);
    await Otp.deleteMany({ email });
    await Otp.create({ email, otp });
    res.status(200).json({ status: true, message: `${messages.otpSent} to ${email}` });
} catch (emailError) {
    console.error("Failed to send OTP email:", emailError);
    res.status(500).json({ status: false, message: "Failed to send OTP email. Please try again later." });
}
```

## Priority
MEDIUM — Users can't log in if email delivery fails silently. Also a UX issue.

issue_2_text:
Security: Message API has no authentication — unauthenticated sender impersonation possible
## Security Vulnerability
The messages API endpoint has no authentication middleware, allowing any user to impersonate any other user and view all messages.

## Affected File
`backend/resources/chatroom/messageListResource.js:14, 31-61`

## Problem

### 1. Unauthenticated message reading
```javascript
// Line 14 — No auth middleware
messageListRouter.get("/", async (req, res) => {
    const allMessages = await Message.find({}).lean();
    // ...
});
```
Anyone can fetch ALL messages in the system without being logged in.

### 2. Sender impersonation via unauthenticated POST
```javascript
// Lines 31-61 — No auth middleware, sender from request body
messageListRouter.post("/", [body("sender").notEmpty()...], async (req, res) => {
    const { sender, content, timestamp } = req.body;
    const newMessage = await Message.create({ sender, content, ... });
    ...
});
```
No authentication required. The `sender` is read directly from the request body, so anyone can forge messages appearing to be from any user.

### 3. Arbitrary deletion
```javascript
messageListRouter.delete("/:id", ...);
```
No auth check — anyone can delete any message by ID.

## Proposed Fix
```javascript
// Add auth middleware to ALL routes
messageListRouter.use(isAuthenticated);

// GET: Filter by authenticated user
messageListRouter.get("/", async (req, res) => {
    const messages = await Message.find({ 
        $or: [{ sender: req.user._id }, { recipient: req.user._id }]
    });
    ...
});

// POST: Derive sender from authenticated user
messageListRouter.post("/", async (req, res) => {
    const { content, recipient, timestamp } = req.body;
    const newMessage = await Message.create({
        sender: req.user._id,  // NOT from request body
        recipient,
        content,
        timestamp: timestamp || new Date(),
    });
    ...
});

// DELETE: Only sender or admin can delete
messageListRouter.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
    const message = await Message.findById(req.params.id);
    if (!message || (message.sender.toString() !== req.user._id.toString() 
        && !req.user.roles.includes('admin'))) {
        return res.status(403).json({ status: false, message: "Unauthorized" });
    }
    ...
});
```

## Severity
CRITICAL — Unauthenticated sender impersonation undermines the entire trust model of the chat system.

